in terms of cross tolerance support, we have the PCA, and the deming regressions

**Main question:** is higher thermal tolerance associated with higher drug tolerance, within or across evolutionary treatment groups?

**Across groups:**
- 40-evolved has consistently higher thermal traits (Topt, Tmax, Th) than 35-evolved
- But drug tolerance shifts are drug-specific: 40-evolved is higher only for caspofungin; 35-evolved actually has higher fluconazole and amphotericin tolerance
- Pooled Deming regression slopes go in opposite directions across drugs (positive for caspofungin, negative for amphotericin and fluconazole) — no consistent cross-tolerance signal
- The pooled Deming slopes are confounded by between-group separation and should not be interpreted as evidence of cross-tolerance

**Within groups:**
- Within-group Spearman correlations (n=18 per group): all non-significant across all thermal trait × drug combinations
- Pooled within-group centered Spearman (variables centered within each group, then pooled to n=36): still all non-significant; closest is th_c × fluconazole (ρ = 0.26, p = 0.13)
- Within-group centered Deming permutation tests (500 permutations): all non-significant (p = 0.10–1.0)
- Within-group centered PCA: th_c, fluconazole, and amphotericin co-load on PC1 (31.3% of within-group variance), pointing in the same direction — the strongest suggestion of a within-group association
  - BUT: this signal is sensitive to two influential strains (35_D4 and 35_B6), which sit at the extreme negative end of PC1 with both low th_c and low drug tolerance; removing them dissolves the co-loading
  - Caspofungin does not participate in this shared axis (PC1 loading ≈ 0)
  - The two main axes of within-group variation are orthogonal: PC1 (thermal deactivation + drug tolerance) and PC2 (Topt vs Tmax shape trade-off, independent of drugs)

**Power:**
- At n=36, power to detect a true ρ of 0.26 is only ~33% (two-sided α = 0.05)
- To reach 80% power for ρ = 0.26 would require n ≈ 114 strains
- The non-significant Spearman results are therefore better described as inconclusive than as evidence against cross-tolerance — the study is underpowered for this specific question
- The PCA is more sensitive than bivariate tests because it extracts shared structure across all variables simultaneously, which is why it picks up a signal the pairwise tests miss

**Conclusion:**
There is a weak, directional within-group signal in the PCA — strains with higher th_c tend to have higher fluconazole and amphotericin tolerance — but it is not confirmed by bivariate tests, which are underpowered at n=18–36 for effects of this size. The signal is also sensitive to two influential strains (35_D4 and 35_B6). The honest framing is that the data are inconclusive on within-group cross-tolerance: thermal and drug tolerance may have evolved largely independently, but we lack the power to rule out a weak association of ρ ~ 0.25–0.35.

