**Manuscript text (plate-normalized IC50 results, uncorrected p-values; updated November 9, 2026):**

Results reflect the exclusion of five poorly fitting dose-response curves (40_E9 and 40_G9 fluconazole, 40_C9 caspofungin, 40_D8 and 40_E11 amphotericin B; one plate each) and report uncorrected *p*-values. Source: `data-processed/supp-table-ic50-pvalues.csv` (11d-MICs.R, section 6b).

After plate normalization, both evolved groups showed modestly elevated fluconazole tolerance relative to the ancestor, whereas neither group differed from the ancestor in caspofungin or amphotericin B tolerance, and the two groups did not differ from each other for any drug (Figure 2G). For fluconazole, IC~50~ was 1.39-fold higher than the ancestor in the 35°C-evolved group (one-sample *t*-test on plate-normalized log~2~[IC~50~/ancestor IC~50~], *n* = 18 populations, *p* = 0.021, 95% CI for fold change: [1.06, 1.82]) and 1.37-fold higher in the 40°C-evolved group (*n* = 18, *p* = 0.013, 95% CI: [1.08, 1.74]); the two groups were nearly identical (40°C/35°C: 0.99-fold; Welch *t*-test, *n* = 18 per group, *p* = 0.934, 95% CI: [0.70, 1.40]). For caspofungin, the 40°C-evolved group showed a marginal, non-significant trend toward elevated tolerance (1.15-fold; *p* = 0.058, 95% CI: [0.99, 1.33]), while the 35°C-evolved group was indistinguishable from the ancestor (1.06-fold; *p* = 0.394, 95% CI: [0.92, 1.21]); the two groups did not differ (40°C/35°C: 1.09-fold; *p* = 0.374, 95% CI: [0.90, 1.32]). Neither group showed a change in amphotericin B tolerance relative to the ancestor (35°C-evolved: 1.06-fold, *p* = 0.327, 95% CI: [0.94, 1.18]; 40°C-evolved: 0.94-fold, *p* = 0.449, 95% CI: [0.78, 1.12]) or relative to each other (40°C/35°C: 0.89-fold; *p* = 0.241, 95% CI: [0.72, 1.09]). The fluconazole results remained significant after Holm correction within drug (35°C-evolved: *p* = 0.043; 40°C-evolved: *p* = 0.039) but not after Holm correction across all nine tests (*p* = 0.170 and 0.116, respectively; Table SX); we therefore interpret them as suggestive evidence of a modest increase in fluconazole tolerance shared by both selection regimes, rather than a temperature-specific effect.

