*Revised manuscript text:*

The first two PCs together explained 60.9% of within-group variance. PC1 (33.1%) was characterized by co-loading of fluconazole tolerance (loading = −0.567), T~h~ (−0.470), amphotericin B tolerance (−0.470), and T~opt~ (−0.425) in the same direction, with caspofungin tolerance loading weakly in the opposite direction (0.234) and CT~max~ contributing negligibly (0.045). PC2 (27.8%) captured an independent axis of TPC shape variation, with CT~max~ (0.701) and T~opt~ (−0.590) loading in opposing directions; amphotericin B tolerance also loaded moderately on this axis (0.370), whereas fluconazole tolerance, caspofungin tolerance, and T~h~ loaded weakly (|loading| ≤ 0.14). The co-loading of T~h~ and T~opt~ with fluconazole and amphotericin B tolerance on PC1 reveals a shared axis of within-group variation spanning thermal performance and drug tolerance, although this axis partly reflects the correlation between the two drug tolerances themselves (within-group *r* = 0.54); pairwise associations between T~h~ and fluconazole (*r* = 0.29) or amphotericin B (*r* = 0.24) tolerance were weaker. Notably, fluconazole and amphotericin B both target ergosterol — fluconazole by inhibiting its synthesis and amphotericin B by binding it directly — while caspofungin, which loaded in the opposite direction on PC1, acts on a mechanistically distinct target, cell wall β-glucan synthesis. This drug-specific pattern maps onto the mechanistic distinction and is consistent with, though not direct evidence for, a shared dependence on membrane ergosterol composition across thermal adaptation and membrane-active antifungal tolerance.